{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790349596,"nonce":"JW4QiyA+ForFjnL8bE+vBybVEQc=","message":"Confirmed on branch aot-reval (main 19697230 plus 8ba7c838, which keeps main unmangled so an AOT build links at all), Mac arm64 and NUC x86_64. compile_llvm (crates/zyntax_cli/src/backends/llvm_aot.rs) lowers with a bare LLVMBackend, never calls run_passes, and builds the TargetMachine for CPU \"generic\" with no features; its opt level reaches codegen only. It also skips the HIR pipeline: the CLI lower_to_hir stops at monomorphize_module and never calls run_interp_safe_opts or run_native_only_opts, so the inliner, the HIR vectorizers and fma_contract never run. That changes answers as well as speed: bench_mandelbrot built AOT prints 112790102 (the ZYNTAX_DISABLE_FMA=1 checksum) where zynml run prints 112789639, on both machines. NUC, release builds, wall seconds, three interleaved runs (prelude-free ports printing through putchar, since zyntax compile cannot build the prelude, e0f4d4a): fib(40) AOT 0.38, the same .ll through opt -O2 and llc -O2 -mcpu=native 0.21, zynml run 0.01; collatz 0.06, 0.03, 0.09; mandelbrot 0.22, 0.22, 0.25. The Mac was too loaded (load average above 60) to time. crates/zyntax_cli/tests/llvm_aot_zynml.rs on aot-reval now drives zyntax compile end to end; the fix can add a case to it.","files":null}]}